---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/release";
---

<Base title="Privacy — Orbit" description="Orbit has no account, no analytics and no server of its own." current="privacy">
  <section class="page">
    <div class="page__head">
      <div class="lead"><h1><b>Privacy.</b></h1> Short, because there's little to say.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="app">The app</h2>
      <p>
        Orbit has no account, no analytics, no crash reporting and no server of its own. Your projects, chats and
        settings stay on your Mac.
      </p>
      <p>It goes online for these, and only these:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Updates.</strong> Once a day it reads a small file from GitHub Releases to see if there's a newer
          version, and downloads it from there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Catalogs and what you install from them.</strong> The lists of agents, language servers and
          extensions, and the things you install, come from where they're published: the ACP registry, Mason's
          registry on GitHub, Zed's extension catalog. Once a day it checks them for updates to what you installed
          (Settings → General → Keep Up to Date turns that off).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grammars.</strong> The first time a file of a language opens, its tree-sitter grammar is downloaded
          from its repository on GitHub and built on your Mac.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Things you ask for.</strong> Git's pull and push, pull requests, pages you open in the preview.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Agents.</strong> The agents you run are their makers' CLIs. They send your prompts and the code they
          read to their providers, exactly as they would in a terminal, under your account and that provider's
          terms.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="site">This site</h2>
      <p>No cookies, no analytics, no tracking. The theme you pick is remembered in your browser and nowhere else.</p>
      <h2 id="code">Check it</h2>
      <p>The code is on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
